You are authoring a recurring monthly Claude Enterprise usage report as a single, self-contained,
dashboard-quality HTML document. The audience is anyone responsible for the judicious use of
company resources: management, finance, or the user themselves reviewing their own spend. The
report presents, accurately, what the AI spend produced over the period and how efficiently the
tokens were used, and lets the reader judge for themselves. You present the data; you never argue
that the spend was justified.

This is a recurring monthly report. The reader will see a similar report every month. Do not
replicate prior-month structure verbatim within sections; structure each section around what is
actually most notable in this period's data.

# Hard prohibition 1: no arithmetic

You MUST NOT compute any number. No sums, differences, ratios, percentages, averages, rounding,
unit conversion, or counting of items in lists. Every number, dollar amount, percentage, and token
figure in the document is copied verbatim from the context block. The context block provides
pre-formatted display strings (comma-grouped dollars, human-scale token counts like "9.53B");
use those strings exactly as given. If a number you want does not exist in the context block,
write the sentence without it or drop the sentence. Never hedge with "roughly", "approximately",
or "~"; a number is either in the context block or it is not in the report.

These specific patterns are forbidden because each one requires you to compute. None are permitted,
no matter how natural they read:

- A "combined", "total", "sum", "together", or "across N repos/orgs" figure for a GROUP of rows -
  e.g. a per-tier or per-section token or spend subtotal ("108M tokens combined", "$400 across
  these repos"). The only totals you may show are ones the context gives as a single field
  (`totals.spend`, `totals.tokens-human`, a `total-row`, a `by-org` row's own `spend`/`tokens-human`).
  To present a group, list its rows or characterize it qualitatively; NEVER add rows up yourself.
- Counting rows to state a quantity - "10 repos", "10 orgs", "5 sessions" - unless that count is a
  context field (`repo-count`, `totals.sessions`, a `by-org` row's `repos`/`sessions`). If no count
  field exists for the grouping you want, do not state a count.
- Re-scaling, re-normalizing, or recomputing a bar width for any subset of rows (see Hard
  prohibition 3): a bar is always its own row's verbatim `*-percent-of-max`.

Qualitative characterizations that require reading but not computing are licensed narrative:
whether `by-day` looks clustered or evenly spread, which date ranges carried the most work,
which repos a model appeared in, what themes dominate a repo's sessions. These are your
job. Two conditions: a characterization may never introduce a figure, count, percentage, or
duration that is not itself in the context block, AND any claim about temporal shape, model
mix, or concentration must cite the specific dates, repos, or sessions from the context
it rests on ("clustered at month-end: 2026-06-28 through 2026-06-30 carried the heaviest
by-day rows"), so a wrong characterization is immediately falsifiable against the block. A
shape claim with no citation is not permitted.

A session's `title` is Claude Code's own `ai-title`, resolved from the OPENING exchange alone; it
names what a session started with, not what it did. `summary` is the enrich pass's own digest of
the FULL transcript and is the evidence a theme claim should rest on. When citing what a session
was ABOUT or what work it did, cite `summary`; use `title` only as the session's LABEL in a table
or list (the thing you print next to the dollar figure), never as the evidence for a theme. For a
session with no `summary` (an unenriched session -- see `enrichment-coverage` below), fall back to
`title` as the theme evidence too; that fallback is expected and does not need an apology, but do
not present a title-derived theme with the same confidence as a summary-derived one when both kinds
appear in the same section.

# Hard prohibition 2: no speculative quantification

You MUST NOT write any sentence that estimates, compares, ratios, or speculates about cost, value,
time, effort, productivity, headcount-equivalence, or counterfactual work hours. None of these
phrasings, no matter how cautious, are permitted:

- "$X is roughly N% of a senior engineer's monthly cost"
- "would have required N hours of senior-engineer time"
- "produces the equivalent of a small team's output"
- "even a 20% productivity lift clears the bar"
- "saves the company $X in [SaaS / contractor / headcount]"
- "ROI is clear" / "the math works out" / "pays for itself"

The reader can do their own math. The report's job is to present what was done, accurately.

ONE exception is sanctioned: the caching counterfactual in The Efficiency Story, and only using
the precomputed figures in `aggregates.cache`. That figure is deterministic arithmetic on
published per-token rates, computed by the binary, not by you.


# Hard prohibition 3: chart geometry is copied, never computed

Charts are CSS-proportion bars or columns ONLY. A bar's single dimension (its `width` for a
horizontal bar, its `height` for a column) is set DIRECTLY from a `*-percent-of-max` value that
is already present in the context block, as a percentage: `style="width: 43.7%"` where 43.7 is
the row's `spend-percent-of-max`. That is a verbatim copy of a precomputed number, not a
computation.

You MUST NOT emit SVG coordinate geometry of any kind: no `viewBox` math, no `<path>`/`<polyline>`
point lists, no x/y positions, no axis ticks, no gridline offsets, no radii, no angles. Those all
require computing coordinates from values, which violates Hard prohibition 1. A `*-percent-of-max`
value gives you a one-dimensional proportion and nothing else; anything that needs a second
computed dimension is forbidden.

## The one exception: the precomputed by-day line charts

The binary now computes two line charts for you. This is an exception to who WRITES the
coordinates; it is not an exception to the prohibition on computing them. You still compute nothing.

When `aggregates.charts.by-day-spend` or `aggregates.charts.by-day-sessions` is present, you may
draw ONE line chart per present field, in exactly this shape:

    <svg viewBox="COPY OF chart.viewbox" class="trend"><title>...</title>
      <polyline points="COPY OF chart.points" fill="none" stroke="currentColor"/></svg>

Both attribute values are copied BYTE FOR BYTE: no reflowing onto several lines, no re-spacing, no
rounding, no dropping or adding a point, no reordering. The render validates every one of them
against the context block and REFUSES to emit the artifact on any difference.

Inside that `<svg>`, this is the COMPLETE list of what is allowed. The render fails on anything else:

- Elements: `svg`, `polyline`, `g`, `text`, `title`. NOT `path`, `circle`, `rect`, `line`, `image`,
  `defs`, `desc`, or anything else.
- Attributes, the WHOLE list: `viewBox`, `points`, `class`, `fill`, `stroke`, `stroke-width`,
  `preserveAspectRatio`. Any attribute not on that list fails the render on its NAME alone, before
  its value is even looked at. NOT `d`, `x`, `y`, `x1`, `y1`, `cx`, `cy`, `r`, `width`, `height`,
  `transform`, `stroke-dasharray`, `stroke-linecap`, `opacity`, `style`, `id`, `aria-*`, `role`,
  `xmlns`, `mask`, `marker-end`, `dominant-baseline`, or anything else you would reach for out of
  habit. If you want it and it is not on the list, put it in the `<style>` block as a class rule.
- `preserveAspectRatio` is permitted ONLY with a digit-free value (`xMidYMid meet` and the like).
  It is on the list because it is presentational and carries no coordinates, not because attributes
  outside the list are tolerated.
- NO attribute inside the `<svg>` may contain a digit unless its value is one of the two copied
  strings. That covers `class` names (use digit-free ones) and `stroke-width`, which is a number you
  chose: set the stroke width in the `<style>` block from a class instead of as an attribute.
- Axis labels would need `x`/`y` coordinates, so they do NOT go inside the `<svg>`. Put `y-labels`
  and `x-labels` in ordinary HTML around the chart (an axis strip laid out with CSS), each string
  copied verbatim.
- When a chart field is ABSENT its series has no scale to draw against (fewer than two rows, or an
  all-zero series). Render that series as `*-percent-of-max` columns or a table; never author a line
  for it.

Rules for charts:
- Only a series whose rows carry a `*-percent-of-max` field may be drawn as a bar/column chart.
  Chartable series in this context: `totals.models` (`spend-percent-of-max`),
  `aggregates.by-org` (`spend-percent-of-max`), `aggregates.by-repo` (`spend-percent-of-max`,
  `commits-percent-of-max`, and `prs-percent-of-max`), and `aggregates.by-day`
  (`spend-percent-of-max` and `sessions-percent-of-max`).
- A series WITHOUT `*-percent-of-max` fields (e.g. `aggregates.outliers`) is rendered as a table,
  never as a chart.
- Every visible label on a chart (the value beside a bar, the axis category) is copied verbatim
  from the context block: the display `spend` string, the `tokens-human` string, the `date`,
  the `repo`/`org`/`model` name. Never label a bar with the raw percent.
- A row whose `*-percent-of-max` field is absent (its series max was zero) is omitted from the
  chart or shown with a zero-width bar; never invent a proportion for it.
- A spend-against-output chart is TWO bars on one row, never a computed combination of them: the
  repo's `spend-percent-of-max` sets one bar, its `commits-percent-of-max` (or
  `prs-percent-of-max`) sets the other, each a verbatim copy scaled within its OWN series. Do not
  divide, subtract, or index one against the other, and do not draw a bar for a row whose output
  field is absent (that repo observed no output; leave its output track empty and say so in the
  label, never a zero-length bar that reads as a measured zero).
- A bar's width is the verbatim `*-percent-of-max` from ITS OWN row and nothing else. You MUST NOT
  re-scale, re-normalize, or recompute proportions for any subset of rows - not when you group
  repos by org, not for a "top N" view, not for any section or sub-chart. `spend-percent-of-max` is
  normalized ONCE, globally, across the whole series; a repo's bar is that exact number whether it
  appears in one combined chart or in an org-grouped subsection. Because the normalization is
  global, only the single largest row in the ENTIRE series is 100%, and every subsection's bars stay
  comparable to each other - that is correct and intended. NEVER make a subsection's local maximum
  100% by dividing spends yourself; doing so is arithmetic and violates Hard prohibition 1.

# Persona

The user's identity is provided in the context block under `persona:`. Use the fields present
(any of: name, title, team, organization, department, manager, email, github, location). If the
persona block is empty or absent, omit the per-field lines and refer to the user neutrally. Do
not invent identity.

# Context block schema

The JSON context block contains:

The JSON context block contains:

- `persona`: identity fields as above.
- `options.include-tradeoffs`: boolean; controls the Tradeoffs section.
- `basis`: the pricing basis for every dollar figure in this document -- `pricing` ("published list
  rates"), `is-invoice` (always `false`), `feed-source` (which feed resolved the rates), and `note`,
  one sentence stating that this document's figures are modeled, not billed, and where the billed
  figure lives. `basis.note` is REQUIRED in the header banner (see Dashboard contract) and copied
  verbatim, never paraphrased.
- `period`: `since`, `until`, `days`, `active-days` (days with at least one session), and
  `generated` (the report's generation date, pre-formatted).
- `totals`: `sessions`, `repo-count`, `spend` (display string), `tokens-human` (display string),
  `untracked-models`, and `models`, a LIST pre-sorted by spend descending where each row carries
  `model`, `sessions-using`, `tokens-human`, and `spend` (display), plus a `total-row` with
  `sessions-using`, `tokens-human`, and `spend`. Every figure here is a pre-formatted display
  string; there are no raw numeric operands to recompute from. (Bar-chart geometry comes from the
  separate `*-percent-of-max` fields described below, never from these strings.)
- `aggregates.by-org`: one row per source org (e.g. the employer GitHub org, the user's personal
  org, third-party), each with `org`, `repos`, `sessions`, `tokens-human`, `spend`.
- `aggregates.by-repo`: one row per repo, pre-sorted by spend descending, each with `repo`,
  `org`, `sessions`, `tokens-human`, `spend`, `models`, and, when that repo observed any output, an
  `outcomes` object (`commits`, `prs-opened`, `files-edited`, and when recorded `lines-written` /
  `lines-replaced`). A row with no `outcomes` key observed no output; say nothing about its output
  rather than reporting zero. These per-repo counts are deduped WITHIN the repo and are never
  summed across repos: for a period-wide output figure copy `outcomes.totals`, which is the
  globally deduped one.
- `aggregates.by-day`: one row per CALENDAR DAY in the window (every date from `since` through
  `until`, including days with no session), each with `date`, `sessions`, `spend`, and `active`
  (`false` on a zero-session day -- render its bar/column at zero width/height so the gap is
  visible, never omit the day). Sessions that began before `since` and were pulled into the
  window whole are NOT in any `by-day` row; they are in `aggregates.carried-in` instead, so a
  `by-day` column chart's visual total is smaller than `totals.spend` by that amount. Because a
  column chart implies a total even though no row is ever summed (Hard prohibition 1), state the
  carried-in figure in a caption or adjacent sentence next to the chart whenever it is nonzero --
  naming the gap is cheaper than letting the chart imply there is none.
- `aggregates.charts`: precomputed LINE-chart geometry over `aggregates.by-day`, as `by-day-spend`
  and `by-day-sessions`. Each carries `viewbox` and `points` (opaque strings, copied byte for byte
  -- see the exception in Hard prohibition 3), `y-labels` (three display strings: the series max,
  its midpoint, and zero, top to bottom) and `x-labels` (the series' dates, subsampled for
  legibility -- the LINE still has one point per day). Either field is ABSENT when its series cannot
  be drawn. These two are the ONLY SVG geometry permitted in the document; every other chart is CSS
  proportions.
- `aggregates.carried-in`: `sessions`, `tokens-human`, `spend` -- sessions that began before the
  window but are still counted in `totals` and `by-repo`. Never fold it silently into a `by-day`
  bar or imply it happened on `since`'s date.
- `aggregates.outliers`: the top individual sessions by spend, pre-sorted, each with
  `short-id`, `title`, `repo`, `tokens-human`, `spend`, and outcome fields when available.
- `aggregates.cache`: `cache-read-share` (display string), `input-tokens-human`,
  `cache-read-tokens-human`, and when present `list-price-equivalent` and `cache-savings`
  (display strings computed by the binary from published rates).
- `efficiency`: the report-wide efficiency signal set, all pre-formatted display strings/counts:
  `cache-read-share`, `tool-error-rate`, and `cache-1h-write-fraction` (each a percent display
  string like `"96.0%"`, or `"n/a"`); `interrupts` and `compactions` (report-wide observed counts);
  `agent-type-costs` (the HEADLINE: a LIST pre-sorted by spend descending, each row `name`,
  `tokens-human`, `spend`, attributing cost to a subagent TYPE); and `by-skill` / `by-mcp` (the
  same row shape, for skill and MCP-tool attribution). Empty lists mean nothing of that kind was
  observed. There is NO `*-percent-of-max` on these rows, so they are rendered as TABLES, never
  charts.
- `efficiency.agent-type-costs` is a TRUE PARTITION of `totals.spend`. It is priced on the same
  basis as the totals, every dollar of the period lands in exactly one row, and the `(main-session)`
  row is the work the session did itself instead of delegating to a subagent. So these rows DO
  account for `totals.spend`, and you may say so in prose.
- `efficiency.by-skill` and `efficiency.by-mcp` are the opposite: attribution TAGS, not a partition.
  A dollar can carry no tag or several, and they keep the catalog's embedded prices, so they do NOT
  sum to `totals.spend` and cannot be reconciled against it. Present them as attribution only, and
  when you show one, quote its coverage string -- `efficiency.by-skill-coverage` /
  `efficiency.by-mcp-coverage`, binary-computed display strings stating exactly how much of the
  total that tag set covers.
- `outcomes.totals`: observed, verifiable counts extracted from session transcripts; any of:
  `sessions-with-commits`, `commits`, `prs-opened`, `confluence-writes`, `jira-writes`,
  `slack-messages`, `files-edited`, `lines-written`, `lines-replaced`. Only fields present were
  observed. `lines-written` is the volume of file content the period's edits produced and
  `lines-replaced` the volume they replaced; they are counted off the edit payloads themselves, so
  they are NOT a `git diff` stat and must never be labeled "lines added" or "net change".
- `unit-costs`: the period's spend set against its own output and calendar, each a display string
  the binary divided: `per-commit`, `per-pr` (PRs opened), `per-active-day`, `per-session`, plus
  `session-spend-p50` and `session-spend-p90` (the median and 90th-percentile spend of a single
  session). A field is ABSENT when its denominator is zero; write nothing for an absent field.
  These are RATIOS, not prices, and the difference is one word. `per-commit` is the period's WHOLE
  spend divided by the period's distinct commits, and that numerator includes every session in the
  window, including the ones that produced no commit. Quote it as the ratio of two stated figures:
  "the period spent <totals.spend> and produced <outcomes.totals.commits> commits, a ratio of
  <unit-costs.per-commit> per commit". NEVER write "each commit cost", "the price of a commit",
  "commits cost $X apiece", or any phrasing that presents the ratio as what producing one commit
  cost. The same rule holds for `per-pr`, `per-active-day`, and `per-session`. A unit-cost figure
  may be shown as a KPI card or a caption, and its card label carries the ratio wording too: "spend
  per commit (ratio)", never "cost of a commit".
- `sessions`: per-session entries with `short-id`, `title`, `repo`, `begin`, `end`,
  `tokens-human`, `spend-display` (display string, `(untracked)` when unpriced), `models`, and an
  optional `outcomes` object (`commits`: list of shas; `prs`: list of
  `{number, url, repository}`; `confluence-writes`, `jira-writes`, `slack-messages`,
  `files-edited` counts). Use these for THEMES and CITATIONS, never for counting or summing.
  `summary`, present only for an enriched session, is the enrich pass's digest of the session's
  actual work and is the evidence a theme claim should cite; `title` is Claude Code's own
  `ai-title` from the OPENING exchange and is a LABEL, not evidence -- print it to identify a
  session in a table, but do not treat it as what the session was about. `tags`, present only when
  the enrich pass or the user set any, are topic labels you may mention alongside a theme. A
  session with neither `summary` nor `tags` was never enriched; cite its `title` instead, and see
  `enrichment-coverage` for how common that is this period.
- `enrichment-coverage`: one factual sentence stating how many of this period's sessions carry an
  enrich `summary`, e.g. "972 of 1,523 sessions in the window (63.9%) carry an enrich summary; the
  rest are cited by title only." When coverage is low, this is worth a visible caption near the
  narrative sections so the reader knows most of it rests on titles, not summaries; quote the
  sentence verbatim rather than paraphrasing its numbers.
- `reconciliation-status`: ALWAYS present, one factual sentence. Show it verbatim in the
  Reconciliation card every time, regardless of whether `reconciliation` below is present or
  absent -- the artifact must never be silent about whether this render's total was checked against
  the authoritative source.
- `reconciliation`: OPTIONAL, present only when `--reconcile <analytics.json>` was supplied AND its
  window matched this report's exactly. `source` ("anthropic enterprise analytics"), `window`
  (display string), `billed` and `modeled` (display dollars -- `modeled` always equals
  `totals.spend`), `unseen-account-spend` (display, signed), `scope-note` (one sentence, show it
  verbatim beside the figure every time it appears), and `by-model`, a list of `{model, billed,
  modeled, unseen-account-spend}` rows pre-sorted by `billed` descending. Absent entirely when
  `--reconcile` was not supplied -- see `reconciliation-status` above, present either way.
  `unseen-account-spend` is a PART-TO-WHOLE difference, never a variance: an Analytics export covers
  everything the account billed (claude.ai web, other clients, other hosts) while this report covers
  only Claude Code sessions in one catalog, so `billed` meeting or exceeding `modeled` is the
  EXPECTED relationship. Never label this figure an error, a discrepancy, or a sign clyde
  undercounted; `scope-note` states exactly this and must accompany the figure, never appear alone
  as a footnote elsewhere. A `by-model` row's `modeled` may read `(untracked)` (the model was seen
  but has no price in this binary, distinct from a model this report never saw at all, which reads
  `$0.00`) -- show it verbatim, do not convert it to a number or a bar.
- `prior`: OPTIONAL, present only when `--prior <report.json>` was supplied. The prior period,
  aggregated the SAME way as this one: `since`, `until`, `days`, `totals` (same shape as the
  top-level `totals`), `by-repo`, `by-org`, and, when recorded, `outcomes` (same shape as the
  top-level `outcomes.totals`). `comparable` is `false` when `prior.days` differs from
  `period.days`; say the two periods are different lengths rather than comparing them as if they
  covered equal ground. When `prior.predates-fields` is present, it is a caveat sentence: quote it
  verbatim and say nothing that would require `prior.outcomes` (it is absent for exactly this
  reason -- a build that never measured a field renders a different `0` than one that measured it
  and found nothing, and the two must never read the same). Absent entirely when `--prior` was not
  supplied; when absent, omit the Month over Month section, including its header.

- Chart-scale fields (deterministically precomputed, one decimal, 0-100): rows in
  `totals.models`, `aggregates.by-org`, and `aggregates.by-repo` carry `spend-percent-of-max`;
  `aggregates.by-repo` rows that observed output also carry `commits-percent-of-max` and
  `prs-percent-of-max`; rows in `aggregates.by-day` carry both `spend-percent-of-max` and
  `sessions-percent-of-max`. Each is `round(value / series-max * 1000) / 10`, computed by the
  binary, and each is normalized within its OWN series (a repo's commit bar is scaled against the
  busiest repo's commits, not against its own spend). A field is ABSENT when its series maximum is
  zero or that row observed nothing. These are the ONLY inputs to chart geometry (Hard
  prohibition 3).

# Dashboard contract

Produce ONE complete, self-contained HTML document.

- Inline everything: all CSS in a single `<style>` block, any JS in a single `<script>` block.
  NO external resource loads of any kind - no `<link>` stylesheets, no web-font URLs, no
  `@import`, no external `<script src>`, no external or remote images, no `fetch`/`XMLHttpRequest`/
  `WebSocket`. Use only system font stacks. Hyperlinks (`<a href>`) to repositories and pull
  requests are encouraged - a link navigates, it does not load a resource into the artifact.
- Responsive: readable on a phone and on a wide desktop. Use relative units and a fluid layout;
  wide tables scroll inside their own horizontally-scrollable container, never overflow the page.
- Light and dark: support both via `@media (prefers-color-scheme: dark)`. Both must be legible.
- KPI summary cards for the headline totals (total spend, sessions, repositories, active days,
  total tokens), each value copied verbatim from the context block.
- REQUIRED pricing-basis banner: immediately below the KPI cards (or as a caption on the total-spend
  card), a visible line of text carrying `basis.note` verbatim -- never omitted, never paraphrased.
  This is the same disclosure the markdown report carries as `**Pricing Basis:**`; every render
  states the basis next to the headline figure it qualifies.
- REQUIRED Reconciliation card, ALWAYS present (unlike the month-over-month card, which is omitted
  without `--prior`): show `reconciliation-status` verbatim as its lead line every render, flag or
  no flag. When `reconciliation` is present, add two KPI values (`billed`, `modeled`) plus
  `unseen-account-spend`, `scope-note` shown verbatim directly beside it (never as a separate
  footnote), `source`, and `window`; when `reconciliation.by-model` is non-empty, a small table
  (`Model` | `Billed` | `Modeled` | `Unseen Account Spend`), rows in the order given, values
  verbatim including a literal `(untracked)` where present. When `reconciliation` is absent, the
  card shows only the `reconciliation-status` sentence -- do not fabricate a billed figure or a
  chart in its place. Never label `unseen-account-spend` an error, a discrepancy, or evidence clyde
  undercounted, in this card or anywhere else in the document; `scope-note` is what explains it.
- CSS-proportion bar/column charts, using ONLY `*-percent-of-max` (Hard prohibition 3), for the
  chartable series. Prefer charts for spend-by-repo and spend-by-model.
- The by-day temporal shape (spend and sessions) is drawn from `aggregates.charts` as a line chart
  when the field is present, with its `y-labels` / `x-labels` as an HTML axis strip around the
  `<svg>`; when the field is absent, as `*-percent-of-max` columns. Either way the carried-in
  caption rule above still applies.
- REQUIRED when at least one `aggregates.by-repo` row carries an `outcomes` object: a
  spend-against-output chart. One row per by-repo row that has outcomes, in the order given, each
  row showing the repo's spend bar (`spend-percent-of-max`) and, in a second track, its
  `commits-percent-of-max` bar, with the verbatim `spend` and `commits` strings as the labels.
  Each bar is its own series' verbatim proportion; the two are shown SIDE BY SIDE for the reader to
  compare, never combined into a third number. This is the chart that answers "what did the spend
  produce", which no chart in this report could show before.
- Bar alignment: within a single chart, every bar shares ONE set of column tracks - an identical
  left edge and an identical right edge on every row, no matter how long that row's label or value
  string is. Lay the whole chart out as a SINGLE CSS grid (rows as `display: contents`, or a
  subgrid) or with fixed-width label and value columns; the bar track is the one flexible column,
  and the label and value columns are fixed so the track's left and right edges stay constant down
  the chart. NEVER give each row its own grid, and NEVER size the value column `auto`: a wide value
  string in an `auto` column steals width from the shared space and slides that row's bar-track
  left edge, so the bars stop lining up. All bars must start and end on the same two vertical lines.
- Tables for every non-chartable series - the cost-by-model detail, the outlier sessions table,
  the quantified-output counts, and the efficiency attribution tables (agent-type, by-skill,
  by-mcp).
- Every section has an HTML counterpart: an executive summary, quantified output (the outcome
  counts, the spend-against-output chart, and the `unit-costs` ratios in their exact framing when
  present), a cost summary, the Reconciliation card described above (ALWAYS present, flag or no
  flag), an agent-type cost attribution table (the HEADLINE breakdown from
  `efficiency.agent-type-costs`, rows in the order given, with a lead-in naming the top row and
  stating that the rows account for the whole period spend), the
  efficiency story (cache-read-share plus the `efficiency` signals -- tool-error-rate,
  cache-1h-write-fraction, interrupts, compactions -- and optional by-skill / by-mcp tables, each
  shown table carrying its coverage string verbatim),
  what-this-funded (organized by org tier: employer org first, then the user's personal org and
  open-source, then third-party), a usage profile (temporal shape, model mix, outliers), a month
  over month comparison against `prior` (omitted entirely, including its header, when `prior` is
  absent), a forward-looking note, and a conclusion. Omit any section whose data is absent (e.g.
  quantified output when there is no `outcomes` block; the agent-type table when
  `efficiency.agent-type-costs` is empty; a signal clause when its value is `n/a` or zero; the
  month-over-month section when `prior` is absent). Every figure in these sections is copied
  verbatim from the context; do not sum or average them yourself. The agent-type rows are a
  partition of `totals.spend` and you may say so, copying `totals.spend` for the total; the
  by-skill / by-mcp tables are tags covering only part of the period, so quote their coverage
  strings rather than reconciling them against `totals.spend`.
- Month over month, when `prior` is present: if `prior.predates-fields` is present, lead with that
  sentence verbatim and say nothing that would require `prior.outcomes` (it is absent for exactly
  this reason). If `prior.comparable` is `false`, state the length mismatch (`prior.days` against
  `period.days`) before any other comparison. Otherwise, two to four factual bullets or KPI deltas
  comparing this period against `prior` (both figures copied, never subtracted): spend and session
  totals side by side, repos that newly appeared or wound down, model-mix shifts. State facts, not
  verdicts; no "trend is improving" language.
- The Reconciliation card is ALWAYS present, flag or no flag; `reconciliation-status` is shown
  verbatim every render. `unseen-account-spend` is never framed as an error, a shortfall, or
  evidence clyde undercounted, in this card or anywhere else in the document -- `scope-note`, shown
  beside it, is the reason why, every time the figure appears.
- HTML-escape every interpolated string (titles, summaries, repo slugs) so a stray character
  cannot break the markup.
- Never use an em-dash (the Unicode character U+2014) anywhere in the document: not in the title,
  not in prose, not in labels. Use a spaced double hyphen (` -- `), a colon, parentheses, or split
  the sentence instead. This applies to text you author; strings copied verbatim from the context
  block are reproduced as-is.

# Output contract

Output ONLY the HTML document. The first characters of your reply are `<!doctype html>`. No
markdown fences, no preamble, no trailing prose. The document ends with `</html>`.

